llama: qwen35 reads the token embedding lazily with LLAMA_LAZY_MODE_ON
Fold-into: upstream/loader

The defect

A model with a device that has no mmap support (HTP0, OpenCL) loads with no file mapping, thus
the token embedding of the CPU takes anonymous memory: 515 MiB for Qwen3.5 2B Q8_0 and 644 MiB
for the 4B. The embedding is a row lookup, and a decode reads one row of 2176 or 2720 bytes for
each token. The loader has a lazy read for such a tensor (TENSOR_READ_LAZY), but qwen35 does not
mark its embedding.

The change

qwen35 creates token_embd.weight with TENSOR_READ_LAZY. With LLAMA_LAZY_MODE_ON the tensor stays
in a file mapping with POSIX_MADV_RANDOM, and a token reads its row on demand. Its pages are file
pages that the kernel can reclaim. The other tensors load as the load mode says. The preset mode
AUTO makes a tensor lazy only above 4 GiB and only when each device has mmap support, thus the
preset load does not change. On a CPU-only model the output is a copy of the embedding in the
model buffer, thus the lazy read saves no memory there.

test-lazy-embd loads the generated qwen35 model with the lazy mode off, and on with the load
modes NONE and MMAP. It requires the same logits in the three loads, and the report of the lazy
read. Without the flag the test fails. On the host (CPU, the 2B and the 4B Q8_0 files, 300 prompt
tokens and 8 decodes) the logits hashes are the same with the lazy mode on and off, in the two
load modes.

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 with the patches of patches/series above this one.

